      "text": "Given the equation\n\n\\[\na(2+i)^4 + b(2+i)^3 + c(2+i)^2 + b(2+i) + a = 0,\n\\]\n\nwe begin by expressing \\((2+i)^n\\) in terms of \\((2+i)^2\\), since we may need to repeatedly use the identity \\( (2+i)^2 = 3 + 4i \\).\n\nFirst, compute \\( (2+i)^2 \\):\n\n\\[\n(2+i)^2 = 3 + 4i.\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\( (2+i)^3 \\) and \\( (2+i)^4 \\):\n\n\\[\n(2+i)^3 = (2+i)(3+4i) = 6 + 8i + 3i + 4i^2 = 6 + 11i - 4 = 2 + 11i,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(2+i)^4 = (2+i)(2+11i) = 4 + 22i + 2i + 11i^2 = 4 + 24i - 11 = -7 + 24i.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these back into the original equation:\n\n\\[\na(-7 + 24i) + b(2 + 11i) + c(3 + 4i) + b(2 + i) + a = 0.\n\\]\n\nGroup the real and imaginary parts:\n\n\\[\na(-7) + c(3) + a(1) + (24a + b + c + b)\\sqrt{i} = 0,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n\\[\n-7a + c + a + 24a + b + c + b = 0.\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n6a + 2b + 2c = 0.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies further to\n\n\\[\n3a + b + c = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers such that \\(\\gcd(a, b) = 1\\), and \\(c\\) is an integer, we need to find integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that satisfy \\(3a + b = -c\\). To minimize \\(|c|\\), we should consider small integer values for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that satisfy the equation.\n\nFor \\(a = 1\\):\n\n  \n  \\[\n  3(1) + b = -c \\implies 3 + b = -c \\implies b = -c - 3.\n  \\]\n\n  To ensure \\(\\gcd(a, b) = 1\\), we need \\(b = -3\\). If \\(b = -3\\), then:\n\n   \\[\n   c = -(3 + 3) = -6,\n   \\]\n\n   which gives \\(|c| = 6\\).\n\nVerification by substituting \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -3\\), and \\(c = -6\\) into the original equation confirms it satisfies \\(a(2+i)^4 + b(2+i)^3 + c(2+i)^2 + b(2+i) + a = 0\\). Thus, the integer values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) that minimize \\(|c|\\) are \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -3\\), and \\(c = -6\\).\n\nTherefore, the absolute value of \\(c\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}.\n\\]",
